PR #2502  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Clarify that mstatus.TW traps WFI in all modes less privileged than M-mode (not just modes lower than the current privilege level), preventing guest/user code from evading WFI interception in virtualized or multi-level privilege environments
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:32-48  mstatus_init
      mstatus_val is initialized to 0 (line 35), which implicitly clears MSTATUS_TW. No code explicitly acknowledges or documents the TW policy. The firmware depends on this zero-init behavior to guarantee TW=0, which is correct (M-mode firmware should not trap WFI from lower modes), but the invariant is invisible: no comment, no assert, no explicit csr_clear(CSR_MSTATUS, MSTATUS_TW). If future code restructures mstatus initialization (e.g., reading-modifying-writing instead of zero-baseline), TW could inadvertently become 1, creating a denial-of-service vector for S/U-mode WFI.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_illegal_insn.c:48-68  system_opcode_insn
      WFI (opcode 0x10500073) dispatches through illegal_insn_table[28] → system_opcode_insn. At line 65, funct3==0 (WFI's funct3) triggers the 'Invalid opcode' check for CSR instructions, falling through to truly_illegal_insn(). WFI is never semantically recognized — no WFI emulation, no power-management awareness, no check of mstatus.TW. Currently harmless because OpenSBI always runs with TW=0 (so WFI never traps here), but if TW were ever 1, WFI would be routed through the illegal-instruction path as an unrecognized opcode rather than handled as a TW-trap requiring explicit WFI emulation or TW clearing.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_illegal_insn.c:23-34  truly_illegal_insn
      The final fallback for WFI when trapped from S/U-mode due to TW=1. Constructs CAUSE_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION trap info with tval=WFI encoding and redirects to lower privilege mode via sbi_trap_redirect(). No WFI-specific handling or power-management policy. If TW were 1, this code path returns the WFI trap to S-mode unchanged — S-mode re-executes WFI, traps again, creating an infinite retrap loop (effective DoS). Currently safe only because TW=0 is always maintained.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_illegal_insn.c:148-175  sbi_illegal_insn_handler
      Entry point for CAUSE_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION trap dispatch. Extracts insn from tval, indexes illegal_insn_table via (insn & 0x7c) >> 2 at line 174. WFI=0x10500073 correctly maps to slot 28 → system_opcode_insn. 16-bit instruction check (lines 166-172) correctly handles the case where tval might hold an address. The dispatch logic is correct for any instruction encoding; the downstream lack of WFI recognition is system_opcode_insn's responsibility, not this dispatcher's.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap.c:103-232  sbi_trap_redirect
      Redirects trap to S-mode (or VS-mode). For WFI trapped via TW=1, sets scause=CAUSE_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION, stval=WFI encoding (lines 202-204), MPP=S-mode (lines 210-211), mepc=S-mode trap vector. After the S-mode handler's sret, WFI re-executes in S-mode. If TW is still 1 in mstatus, this creates an infinite retrap loop because M-mode never clears TW or emulates WFI before redirecting. The redirect path makes no attempt to detect or break the WFI→TW→illegal_insn→redirect→WFI cycle. Currently safe only because TW=0 prevents this path from being exercised for WFI.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap.c:327-331  sbi_trap_handler
      Top-level trap dispatcher correctly routes CAUSE_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION to sbi_illegal_insn_handler() at lines 328-331. No pre-dispatch check of mstatus.TW occurs, but given TW=0 is the firmware's policy, no WFI traps arrive here. The dispatch structure is correct; additional TW-awareness would be a defense-in-depth improvement but is not required for correctness under current policy.
  [OK] include/sbi/riscv_encoding.h:33-33  
      MSTATUS_TW = 0x00200000 is correctly defined. The constant exists but is never referenced by any C code in OpenSBI (no csr_set/csr_clear on CSR_MSTATUS with this bit). The unused definition confirms the TW-agnostic design but is not itself a bug — having the constant available is good practice for future use. The design choice to not use it is the potential_risk (flagged at mstatus_init), not the constant definition itself.
  [RISK] include/sbi/riscv_encoding.h:79-79  
      HSTATUS_VTW = 0x00200000 defined — the hypervisor-level WFI trap control for VS/VU-mode. HSTATUS is initialized to 0 in mstatus_init (sbi_hart.c:87), so VTW defaults to 0. This means that if mstatus.TW=1 (hypothetical), VS/VU-mode WFI traps directly to M-mode rather than to HS-mode, because HS-mode's hstatus.VTW=0 does not intercept it first. This violates the hypervisor's expectation that it can independently control WFI trapping for its virtualized guests. Currently moot because TW=0 always, but represents an unexplored interaction between the two trap controls.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:212-252  delegate_traps
      Correctly does NOT delegate CAUSE_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION to S-mode (lines 225-231 enumerate only MISALIGNED_FETCH, BREAKPOINT, USER_ECALL, PAGE_FAULTS). This matches the RISC-V privileged spec requirement that illegal instruction traps must not be delegated. As a result, any WFI traps from TW=1 (hypothetical) would be handled in M-mode first. Combined with the lack of WFI recognition in the M-mode handler, this means M-mode intercepts every S/U-mode WFI but does nothing useful — the problem is in the handler, not in the delegation policy.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:794-807  sbi_hart_switch_mode
      Reads current mstatus at line 794, modifies MPP/MPIE/MPV fields via INSERT_FIELD, writes back at line 807. This preserves whatever TW value is currently in the hardware mstatus CSR. Since OpenSBI always maintains TW=0 (via mstatus_init at boot/reinit), the preserved TW is always 0. When switching to S-mode with TW=0, the S-mode payload can use WFI freely without trapping. This is correct behavior under the current policy.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:711-721  sbi_hart_reinit
      Called during warm resume from suspend. Re-calls mstatus_init() at line 715, which clears TW to 0 via zero-init. This correctly restores a known-good mstatus state after resume. If TW was 0 before suspend (which is the only state OpenSBI ever sets), it remains 0. If an external agent had set TW=1 before suspend, reinit correctly clears it — this is arguably the desired defensive behavior, restoring the firmware's invariant.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:148-151  switch_to_next_domain_context
      Saves/restores the full trap context (struct sbi_trap_context) via sbi_memcpy at lines 150-151. This includes regs.mstatus and regs.mstatusH. If a domain was interrupted mid-trap with TW=1 in the saved mstatus field (from a prior boot stage or a corrupted trap frame), the domain context switch preserves and later restores that TW=1 value into the active trap context. When the trap handler returns via mret, the TW=1 value enters the hardware mstatus CSR. This is the only code path in OpenSBI that could inject a non-zero TW into mstatus without going through mstatus_init. No validation or sanitization of mstatus fields occurs during the context save/restore.
  [OK] include/sbi/riscv_asm.h:183-186  wfi
      wfi() macro correctly emits the WFI instruction with a compiler memory barrier. All callers in OpenSBI execute in M-mode where mstatus.TW does not apply per the RISC-V privileged spec (TW traps WFI from modes less privileged than M). The macro itself is correctly implemented; the absence of S/U-mode WFI emulation is a higher-level design issue, not a defect in this macro.
  [OK] include/sbi/riscv_encoding.h:959-960  
      INSN_MASK_WFI (0xffffff00) and INSN_MATCH_WFI (0x10500000) are correctly defined. These constants encode the full 32-bit WFI matching pattern (WFI has no variable fields besides the base opcode). The illegal-instruction handler uses opcode-based table indexing rather than these mask/match constants, but the definitions are architecturally correct and represent the encoding knowledge available in the codebase for potential future WFI-aware handling.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hsm.c:182-182  sbi_hsm_hart_wait
      Uses wfi() at line 182 in M-mode while waiting for start signal (IPI). M-mode WFI is immune to mstatus.TW per the RISC-V privileged spec. This is a correct use of WFI for M-mode idle waiting. The broader architectural asymmetry — OpenSBI uses WFI internally in M-mode but provides no WFI emulation or passthrough for lower privilege modes — is a design concern but not a defect in this specific call site.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hsm.c:403-403  __sbi_hsm_suspend_default
      Uses wfi() at line 403 in the default M-mode suspend handler. TW-immune because execution is in M-mode. Correct usage. Represents the M-mode 'wait for interrupt' policy — contrast with S-mode code that relies on TW=0 to safely execute WFI.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:761-765  sbi_hart_hang
      M-mode infinite idle loop using wfi() in a while(1) loop. Safe because mstatus.TW does not apply to M-mode execution. Correctly marked __attribute__((noreturn)). Represents OpenSBI's default wait-forever policy in M-mode, contrasting with S-mode code that depends on TW=0 for WFI to function.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_system.c:119-119  sbi_system_suspend_test_suspend
      Uses wfi() at line 119 in M-mode context (system suspend test path). TW-immune. Included for completeness as an M-mode WFI call site. No issue at this location.
  [OK] firmware/fw_base.S:449-452  _start_hang
      Low-level M-mode hang loop using WFI instruction directly in assembly. Used when early boot fails. Safe because execution is in M-mode where TW does not apply. The WFI;j loop is the standard RISC-V M-mode halt pattern. Sets precedent of WFI usage without TW awareness at the lowest firmware level, which is architecturally correct since this is M-mode code.

